---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About — The Nixon Index"
  description="What The Nixon Index is, who keeps it, and the tradition it works in."
>
  <div class="eyebrow">Colophon</div>
  <h2 class="page-title">About the Index</h2>

  <p>
    The Nixon Index scores public-corruption cases against one fixed formula,
    published before any case is ruled. The standard unit is the Nixon. The
    reference artifact is Watergate, scored retroactively at 100. Every input
    cites a public record, and the arithmetic is shown on every ruling. The
    scores are opinions derived from that method; the records they rest on are
    listed so anyone can check them.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Index takes no party. A ruling that spares one side and a ruling that
    indicts it are produced by the same five factors and the same weights. The
    method is the argument.
  </p>

  <div class="dedication">
    <p class="dedication-lead">Dedicated to</p>
    <p class="dedication-names">
      Howard Zinn,<br />
      Bob Woodward &amp; Carl Bernstein
    </p>
    <p class="dedication-note">
      who held that the record, set down plainly and in full, is enough.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Corrections and disputes are welcome and are the point of publishing the
    inputs. If a record cited here is wrong, incomplete, or has been
    superseded, write to <a href="mailto:nixonindex@gmail.com">nixonindex@gmail.com</a> with the 
    document. Rulings are revised in public, and revisions get their own
    entries.
  </p>

  <p class="credit mono-note">
    Kept by the Bureau of Corruption Metrology ·
    <a href="https://x.com/nixonindex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">x.com/nixonindex</a> ·
    <a href="mailto:nixonindex@gmail.com">nixonindex@gmail.com</a>
  </p>
</Base>

